Google Cloud Storage (GCS) Virtual File System for Midnight Commander
======================================================================

Overview
--------
The GCS VFS plugin lets you browse and manipulate Google Cloud Storage
buckets directly in Midnight Commander panels using the gcs:// prefix.

It shells out to `gcloud storage` CLI commands, so no additional
libraries are needed -- just an authenticated gcloud installation.

Prerequisites
-------------
1. Google Cloud SDK (`gcloud`) installed and in PATH
2. Authenticated: `gcloud auth login`
3. A GCS bucket you have access to

For read-only access to public buckets, authentication is sufficient
(no billing account required).

Build
-----
Build dependencies (Ubuntu/Debian):

    sudo apt-get install -y autoconf automake libtool pkg-config \
        gettext libglib2.0-dev libslang2-dev

Build dependencies (macOS with Homebrew):

    brew install autoconf automake libtool pkg-config gettext \
        glib s-lang

Build and install:

    cd ~/git/mc
    ./autogen.sh
    ./configure --enable-vfs-gcs --prefix=$HOME/.local/mc-gcs
    make -j$(nproc)
    make install

To run as a standalone command:

    cat > ~/.local/bin/mc-gcs << 'EOF'
    #!/bin/bash
    export MC_DATADIR="$HOME/.local/mc-gcs/share/mc/"
    exec "$HOME/.local/mc-gcs/bin/mc" "$@"
    EOF
    chmod +x ~/.local/bin/mc-gcs

Usage
-----
In MC command line:

    cd gcs://BUCKET-NAME          Browse a bucket root
    cd gcs://BUCKET-NAME/path/    Browse a subdirectory

Key bindings (standard MC):

    F3      View a file (downloads to temp, opens viewer)
    F4      Edit a file (downloads to temp, uploads on save)
    F5      Copy files/directories
    F6      Move/rename files/directories
    F7      Create directory
    F8      Delete files/directories
    Ctrl+O  Toggle console to see gcloud command output

All operations work between:
  - Local filesystem <-> GCS
  - GCS <-> GCS (same or different buckets)

How It Works
------------
The plugin registers as a VFS class with prefix "gcs". MC paths like
gcs://bucket/path are parsed with host=bucket, path=/path.

Directory listing:
  Uses `gcloud storage ls -l gs://BUCKET/path/` and parses the output
  to create directory entries. Listings are cached for 30 seconds.

File view/edit:
  Downloads via `gcloud storage cp gs://... /tmp/mc-gcsXXXXXX`.
  On save, uploads back via `gcloud storage cp /tmp/... gs://...`.

Bulk operations (F5/F6/F8):
  When source or destination is a GCS path, MC intercepts the operation
  and runs a single gcloud command instead of recursing file-by-file:

  Copy file:       gcloud storage cp 'gs://src' 'gs://dst'
  Copy directory:  gcloud storage cp -r 'gs://src' 'gs://dst'
  Copy+rename dir: gcloud storage rsync -r 'gs://src' 'gs://dst'
  Move file:       gcloud storage mv 'gs://src' 'gs://dst'
  Move directory:  gcloud storage mv -r 'gs://src' 'gs://dst'
  Move+rename dir: gcloud storage rsync -r 'gs://src' 'gs://dst'
                   && gcloud storage rm -r 'gs://src'
  Delete file:     gcloud storage rm 'gs://path'
  Delete dir:      gcloud storage rm -r 'gs://path'
  Mkdir:           gcloud storage cp /dev/null 'gs://path/dir/'

  The "rename" case (when the user edits the destination name in the
  copy/move dialog) uses `rsync -r` instead of `cp -r` because
  `cp -r src dst` nests src inside dst, while `rsync -r src dst`
  copies the contents directly into dst.

  All bulk commands run via `shell_execute()`, so their output is
  visible in the MC console (Ctrl+O).

Single-item operations (VFS callbacks):
  unlink, rename, mkdir, rmdir use `gcs_run_command()` which captures
  output internally via `mc_popen`/`mc_pread`.

Public Buckets
--------------
You can browse publicly accessible buckets without a billing account.
For example, to browse the Cloud Storage public datasets:

    cd gcs://gcp-public-data-landsat

Limitations
-----------
- Requires `gcloud` CLI in PATH
- No streaming -- files are downloaded entirely before viewing
- Directory creation is emulated (GCS has no real directories)
- File permissions are not preserved (GCS does not support Unix perms)
- Symlinks are not supported
- Progress is shown in the console (Ctrl+O), not in a dialog

Architecture
------------
Source files in src/vfs/gcs/:

  gcs.c             VFS registration, archive management, utilities
  gcs.h             Public header
  internal.h        Shared internal declarations
  gcs_dir.c         Lazy directory listing (gcloud storage ls)
  gcs_file.c        File download/upload, unlink/rename/mkdir/rmdir
  gcs_ops.c         Bulk copy/move/delete with path conversion
  gcs_progress_ui.c Command execution via shell_execute()

Tests in tests/src/vfs/gcs/:

  gcs_build_gs_url.c  Tests for gs:// URL construction
